The text you will be analyzing is as follows: "In this section, we present one of our main contributions: showing that HNSW is a HENN graph, with a high probability. Building on this connection, we derive probabilistic time guarantees for HNSW. We begin with a simplified description of HNSW [46]. During preprocessing, points are inserted incrementally into the index. For each new point, a random variable determines its maximum layer, with the probability of assignment to higher layers decreasing exponentially. After the layers are determined, the point is connected at each level to its nearest neighbors among the previously inserted points, identified using the greedy search procedure (Algorithm 1). Equivalently, HNSW can be viewed as starting from a base layer containing all points and recursively constructing higher layers by randomly sampling subsets of points, such that the size of each layer decreases exponentially with the level. At each layer, an NSW graph [47] is built on the sampled subset. Comparing this construction with the definition of HENN,9we observe that HNSW satisfies the HENN property with high probability, where NSW serves as the underlying proximity graph and the \u03b5-net construction is realized through random sampling (cf. Theorem e1)."

